// components/PostsList.js
import React from 'react';
import { useSelector, useDispatch } from 'react-redux';
import { fetchPosts } from '../actions';

function PostsList() {
  const dispatch = useDispatch();
  const posts = useSelector(state => state.posts);
  const loading = useSelector(state => state.loading);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    dispatch(fetchPosts());
  }, [dispatch]);

  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;

  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map(post => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

export default PostsList;
```

In this example, we have a Redux store with three reducers: `posts`, `users`, and `comments`. The `App` component wraps the `PostsList` component with the `Provider` component from `react-redux`, which makes the Redux store available to all components in the app.

The `PostsList` component uses the `useSelector` hook to select the `posts` and `loading` state from the Redux store, and the `useDispatch` hook to dispatch the `fetchPosts` action. When the component mounts, it dispatches the `fetchPosts` action to fetch the posts from the API.

This is a basic example of how to use Redux with React. In a real-world application, you would likely have more complex reducers, actions, and selectors, and you might also use middleware like Redux Thunk or Redux Saga to handle asynchronous actions.

